WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.660
std::shared_ptr makes reference-count ownership operations safe to use across threads, but that does not make the pointed-to object itself safe for concurrent access.

2
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:22.280
The control block can manage lifetime while the object still needs synchronization, immutability, thread confinement, message passing, or another concurrency strategy.

3
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.420
Lifetime safety and data-race safety are related, but they are not the same guarantee.

4
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:33.780
Capturing smart pointers in threads, tasks, callbacks, and asynchronous continuations is common.

5
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.000
Capturing a shared_ptr can intentionally keep an object alive until work finishes.

6
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.180
That can be the right design when the work requires the object to exist.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:51.600
It can also delay cleanup, retain sensitive state longer than intended, or create cycles when callbacks hold owners that also own the callback.

8
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.040
Weak observation is often useful in asynchronous code.

9
00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:03.500
A task can store a weak_ptr , lock it when it runs, and skip work if the target no longer exists.

10
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.880
This supports cancellation-friendly behavior, but it still needs clear handling for expiration and still needs synchronization for mutable state.

11
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.480
Concurrent systems need documented ownership and shutdown rules.

12
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.420
Who owns work while it is queued?

13
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.600
What happens during cancellation?

14
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.740
Can callbacks outlive their registrants?

15
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.240
What protects shared state?

16
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:32.540
Tests should cover delayed execution, shutdown order, cancellation paths, and repeated registration.

17
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.520
Smart pointers help express lifetime, but they do not replace a concurrency design.
